lless and  meaning there's no comfort to be found  in barry as every laugh is tinged with  darkness and that's what makes it so  good as we've said dark comedy has long  existed in various forms but in modern  days it seems to pop up during times of  political and social turbulence like the  1960s or you know right now barry  unveils the violence of our times while  also showing how it's papered over by  mass media conglomerates who feed us  hackney dishonest narratives no matter  how funny their premise may be but then  she turns him to stone but then she's  still horny so she sucks and then eats  his dick it renders death vivid in a  time when our only exposure to our own  mortality comes via the 24-hour news  cycle at the same time it forces us to  reckon with america's culture of  violence all while being funny to  summarize a socrates line from plato's  symposium real poets use tragedy and  comedy both at once and human life is  experienced as a mix of the tragic and  comedic we'd argue that makes hater and  berg nothing less than contemporary  poets but what do you guys think does  barry straddle tragedy and comedy while  keeping its funny bone intact or has the  show gone too dark for chuckling let us  know in the comments thanks to our  patrons for all your support and be sure  to check out our revamped patreon page  shoot that subscribe button like there's  a tiger in the other room and don't  forget to ring that bell and as always  thanks for watching  later  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you


